From 1bddf67ce8b7d51d93f179673a3fa795a56f5f92 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 08:09:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] pm: HERMES_PM_ACCOUNT_SEED=0 opt-out (set by
 setup-pm), per-lock uv seed marker, one hash per seeded install

Follow-up to #1895 (account-seed hardlinks), its review's five non-blocking points:

1. CI and the live uv cache. A self-hosted runner whose login has a live ~/.hermes
   on the same filesystem resolved uv-cache-path to that account's cache, which the
   setup-pm action then restores/saves with actions/cache and prunes with
   --exact-lock. HERMES_PM_ACCOUNT_SEED=0 (pm.environments.account_seed_enabled; also
   honoured by run_tests.sh's tool-store borrowing) turns borrowing off; setup-pm's
   setup_toolchain.py exports it to every later step and sets it for its own phases.
   The mini's hermes-state lane (runner = the daveey login, HOME=/Users/me, same
   APFS device 16777231 as RUNNER_TEMP, live ~/.hermes/cache/uv -> uv.noindex) does
   not use setup-pm: no cache action, no prune. It keeps borrowing; its comment now
   says what it shares and why that is safe instead of "never touches ~/.hermes".
2. A primary home outside ~/.hermes beside a stale ~/.hermes: documented in
   account_home_root and pm/AGENTS.md; the same opt-out keeps its state its own.
3. The uv cache's .seeded marker keeps one line per lock (newest first, at most 8),
   written atomically, so homes on different uv.lock digests sharing one cache no
   longer re-run each other's payload merge. A payload seed still supersedes its own
   lock's older payload suffix (the +payload -> +payload2 heal).
4. pm/AGENTS.md states the invariant: published tool entries and snapshot files are
   never written in place; _heal_exec_bit's +x is the one sanctioned change. Tests
   catch a borrower's install/heal/re-pin or a snapshot rewrite changing an account
   inode's bytes.
5. A seeded install records the digest it verified (pm.store.verified_digest)
   instead of hashing the published tree again; a staged repair drops it.

tests/pm/test_account_seed.py: 12 new tests; on main 6 fail (opt-out x4, marker,
single hash); the two invariant guards pass on main and each kills a mutant (a
linked root pyproject.toml, an in-place append in _heal_exec_bit).
